  sg/penal-code-1871 — Singapore Penal Code 1871, sections 22-25, 301, 321, 323A,
  378-384, 390-394, 405-406, 415-420 and 503-506, and Criminal Procedure Code
  2010 sections 123-126, published by the Attorney-General's Chambers on
  Singapore Statutes Online; and seven Singapore judgments retrieved from
  the lawplain corpus, whose charges are quoted verbatim. The applicable
  source terms are UNDETERMINED, on the same open question as sg/pdpa-2012; see
  that row's SOURCE-LICENSE.md. Unlike sg/pdpa-2012 this encoding reproduces its
  section VERBATIM rather than paraphrasing it, which is a material difference
  for whatever assessment settles the question. No attribution is recorded
  because none has yet been established, not because none is required.

  sg/penal-code-1871, row legalese-whole-code — the same Code, as consolidated
  on Singapore Statutes Online ("Current version as at 09 Sep 2026"), read from
  the subject's registers/source-bundle. This row quotes the sections it
  encodes VERBATIM, in comments and as inline strings; how much of the Code
  that amounts to has not been measured. The row legalese-aswathy records
  a standing reproduction grant in SSO's Terms of Use cl. 13, with a required
  attribution; the row legalese records the terms as UNDETERMINED. This row has
  not read the Terms itself; see its SOURCE-LICENSE.md, which carries the
  cl. 13(a) attribution in case the first reading is right.

  doctrine/contract/unilateral-mistake — Singapore judgments (Chwee Kin Keong
  and others v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2; Quoine Pte Ltd v B2C2 Ltd
  [2020] SGCA(I) 02), retrieved from eLitigation and published by the Singapore
  Judiciary. The applicable source terms are UNDETERMINED, on the same open
  question as sg/pdpa-2012 and sg/penal-code-1871; see that row's
  SOURCE-LICENSE.md. Like sg/penal-code-1871 and unlike sg/pdpa-2012, this row
  reproduces its sources VERBATIM and at length -- an encoding of a judge-made
  rule that paraphrased the judgment could not be checked against it -- which is
  a material fact for whatever assessment settles the question. No attribution
  is recorded because none has yet been established, not because none is
  required.

  That row also quotes two things whose terms are NOT the Singapore question,
  and they are recorded here because a blanket statement would be wrong about
  both. Restatement (Second) of Contracts ss 153 and 154 are a copyrighted work
  of the American Law Institute; this repository does not reproduce them from
  the Institute's publication, but quotes them as they appear in Donovan v RRL
  Corp (2001) 26 Cal 4th 261, an opinion of the Supreme Court of California,
  which is not subject to copyright. The English and Australian authorities the
  row discusses are not reproduced from their own reports at all: every
  quotation of them is a quotation of the Singapore Court of Appeal's account,
  and so falls under the Singapore question above.

  il/hvac-work-licensing-2025 — Israeli primary legislation (the Regulation of
  Engagement in Work on Refrigeration or Air-Conditioning Systems Law 5785-2025,
  Sefer HaChukim 3349) and subsidiary legislation (its Fees Regulations, Kovetz
  HaTakanot 5785 p. 2116 and 5786 p. 2062), read from the Open Book of Laws on Hebrew Wikisource
  with the gazette PDF served by gov.il deposited beside it. The applicable
  source terms are UNDETERMINED, on the same question as il/penal-law-1977; see
  that row's SOURCE-LICENSE.md. The row also transcribes, in five lines of
  comment, the text of a DRAFT of those Regulations as it appears in a figure of
  an academic article (Schwartz, Bar-Siman-Tov & Gelbard, 8 Law, Society and
  Culture 353 (2025)); the figure is not reproduced. No attribution is recorded
  because none has yet been established, not because none is required.

  il/traffic-ordinance-bac — Israeli primary legislation (the Traffic Ordinance
  [New Version] 5721-1961, s.64B(a) and s.62(3), and the Traffic Ordinance
  Amendment (No. 97) Law 5771-2010, Sefer HaChukim 2265 p. 91) and subsidiary
